{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"characters","title":"Characters, ASCII & DIY String Functions","group":"Natural Language Processing","order":17,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every character is a number. Knowing the codes lets you build string functions yourself — a common interview exercise and the basis of text encoding.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>ord() and chr()</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>text = \"The quick brown fox\"\nprint([ord(c) for c in \"Tq \"])                    # [84, 113, 32]\nprint(chr(97), chr(65), chr(93))                  # a A ]\ncodes = [ord(c) for c in text]\nprint(\"\".join(chr(n) for n in codes) == text)     # True\nprint(\"\".join(chr(n) for n in range(65, 91)))     # ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\nprint(\"\".join(chr(n) for n in range(90, 64, -1))) # ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBA</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>Characters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>48–57</td><td><code>0</code>–<code>9</code></td></tr><tr><td>65–90</td><td><code>A</code>–<code>Z</code></td></tr><tr><td>97–122</td><td><code>a</code>–<code>z</code> (each lowercase letter = its uppercase + 32)</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>space</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Correction</div><p>The notebook prints <code>ascii</code> instead of its list <code>ascii_</code> and gets <code>&lt;built-in function ascii&gt;</code> — <code>ascii</code> is a built-in function. Avoid names that shadow built-ins (<code>list</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>ascii</code>…).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>DIY upper, lower and title</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def upper(text):\n    return \"\".join(chr(ord(c) - 32) if \"a\" &lt;= c &lt;= \"z\" else c for c in text)\n\ndef lower(text):\n    return \"\".join(chr(ord(c) + 32) if \"A\" &lt;= c &lt;= \"Z\" else c for c in text)\n\ndef title(text):\n    return \" \".join(upper(w[:1]) + lower(w[1:]) for w in text.split(\" \"))\n\nprint(upper(\"delhi\"), lower(\"CARTOON\"))               # DELHI cartoon\nprint(title(\"let us study machine learning today\"))   # Let Us Study Machine Learning Today\nprint(title(\"hello  world\"))                          # Hello  World</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>w[:1]</code> instead of <code>w[0]</code> keeps <code>title</code> from crashing on empty words (two spaces in a row).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i>DIY isalpha and isdigit</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def is_alpha(text):\n    return len(text) &gt; 0 and all(\"A\" &lt;= c &lt;= \"Z\" or \"a\" &lt;= c &lt;= \"z\" for c in text)\n\ndef is_digit(text):\n    return len(text) &gt; 0 and all(\"0\" &lt;= c &lt;= \"9\" for c in text)\n\nprint(is_alpha(\"datascience\"), is_alpha(\"1234\"))     # True False\nprint(is_digit(\"1234\"), is_digit(\"machinelearning\")) # True False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Bug in the notebook</div><p>The notebook's <code>check_alpha</code> returns <code>False</code> for <code>'datascience'</code> — its <code>if len(text) == c</code> check sits <strong>inside</strong> the loop, so it decides after the first character. Check after the loop (or use <code>all()</code> as above).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Finding runs of digits and words</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>txt = \"The number is 8372493 not 423423\"\nruns, current = [], \"\"\nfor ch in txt:\n    if ch.isdigit():\n        current += ch\n    elif current:\n        runs.append(current)\n        current = \"\"\nif current:\n    runs.append(current)\nprint(runs)                                     # ['8372493', '423423']\n\nsentence = \"GFG is launching a new Data Structure in Python Course\"\nprint([w for w in sentence.split() if w.lower().startswith(\"i\")])   # ['is', 'in']\nprint([w for w in sentence.split() if w.lower().endswith(\"e\")])     # ['Structure', 'Course']\nprint(\"let us study data science\".find(\"data\"))                     # 13</code></pre></figure></section>"}